 "verdict": "display head: it has text above it on every page it appears on, so it marks where a block begins",
 "what": "where on the printed page each PG003 paraphrase head sits, measured from the scan's own per-word bounding boxes",
 "why": "recover_pg003_heads.py treats PARAPHRASIS PACHYMERAE (N) as the point a paraphrase block begins. If it were a running head instead, every boundary taken from one would be wrong the same way, putting the switch at a page top when the text changes hands on the page before. Two things in the text OCR suggest exactly that: a number that comes back on a later page, and one head split across a spread with a column number between its halves. Position decides it and the two readings do not.",
